A||N||9||Bikemanj||Limited Slip for Getrag||||5||
Z||000000||Bikemanj||12-09-2001||03:32 AM||||I have an '88 Formula and I am about to do a S/C 3800 swap with the 5 speed. I was wondering if anyone makes limited slip differentials for the Getrag. If so who, and where can I find them? Any help would be appreciated. Also, how hard is it to install a differential?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||12-09-2001||03:36 AM||||Hey Bikemanj!<BR>They are available from Isuzu but they are about $1000...<BR>From what I have heard, they are not needed...<BR>But ask Archie for the facts!<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000002||Bikemanj||12-09-2001||11:51 PM||||Thanks.<BR>Why are they not needed?  I have heard that before but it was never explained.<BR>Any info is appreciated.<BR>thanks||||reg||5
Z||000003||DRH||12-10-2001||12:19 AM||||There was some discussion on here a month or so ago about getting them from a company called Phantom Grip. The price was $300 to $400 and there was some discussion whether it was the Isuzu or the Getrag, but I'm not sure what the final conclusion was.<P>Even though the stock Fiero is not limited slip, it has a tendency to hook up both wheels when accelerating in a straight line. I'm not sure of the exact physics involved, but it makes the LS less important than on traditional rear wheel drive cars.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Neal||12-10-2001||12:38 AM||||Whether or not a LSD is needed all depends on your application.<BR>Real basic physics of it is that a traditional rear drive layout causes a twist in the drivetrain, loading one wheel while unloading the other, and the unloaded wheel will have a tendancy to spin.  A LSD will allow both wheels to hook up, this of course is straight line acceleration.<P>If you taking real hard corners there is a similar loaded/unloaded wheel and the unloaded wheel will spin, creating a need for LSD.<P>Fieros are gonna be ok for the straight line, untill HIGH hp applications are used, but if your serious about racing a road course, or autocross then and LSD will be needed.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Bikemanj||12-10-2001||01:14 AM||||I am definitely serious about getting the car on a road course. The goal is to have the car corner better than stock AND have the power that it should have come with from the factory.<BR>Do you know how many different differentials are available and which might be the best?||||reg||10
Z||000006||cadero2dmax||12-10-2001||03:45 AM||||My Q4 racecar had the very expensive and rare Rocketsports Getrag, a 3.94 ratio with a Thorsen-Gleason diffy built in. These were raced in the Road Racing Achievas in the early/mid 90's, and on one limited edition model of the Achieva.<P> It worked great on the strip (3500RPM and dump the clutch. No tire squeel, or even chirp. The car literally jumped off the line - - I was doing 1,7 60' times in a mid 13's car - that is how hard it launched). The ProSolo launch was also very good. <P>But get into an autocross, or the rest of the ProSolo, (after the launch), or on a track - and I found one thing to be true of LSD's in competition on a rear or mid-engined car. <P>It pushes like a pig!! It doesn't want to turn at all. A limited slip adds/amplifies<BR>so much understeer that I had to completely redo my suspension. <P>After I installed this tranny/LSD, I had to soften the front (I put rubber bushings back on the links of my 1 1/4" swaybar). Plus I had to stiffen the rear (went up 50# on the springs). I had to decrease my caster to allow easier turn-in. I even had to run different front tire pressures in the <BR>20x13x9 1/2 Hoosier slicks.<P>I agree that an LSD is not needed in most applications. On the Grassroots Challenge Cadero, I am not going to have one with a 4.9 and TH 125 (2.84 ratio) trans. <P>I have had all the mid engined excitement an LSD can offer, now it is somebody elses turn to fight it.<P>I do not agree that one is needed in competition - - been there, done that!!<BR> <P>------------------<BR>Injected 4.9 Cad powered 85 SE (Daily Driver)5 speed ('88 cradle)<P>86 carburated 4.9 automatic Grassroots Motorsports Magazine $2002 Challenge Fiero coupe (2.86:1 TH-125 w/shift kit) all on an '88 cradle<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)<P>George Ryan||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bikemanj||12-10-2001||11:52 AM||||Thanks for the info. I think I'll try running the open differential first and see if I really need an LSD. I can definitely use the money on other parts of the car. ||||reg||6
Z||000008||artherd||12-10-2001||04:24 PM||||There are some problems with the Torsen units, mainly I belive their accell lockup is far far too 'tight' (they were designed for a *FWD* car, remenber?)  Anyway, their torque bias under power is too high.  So high infact that Team Python managed to grenade one (hiiigh lockup fighting some big slicks and a v8)<P>George, was this the same unit as yours? I don't remenber.<P><BR>I think we (fieros) may have more luck with a more conventional clutchpack non-biasing LSD, this should allow far more 'slip' and be more consistant.<P>FWD LSDs are NOT the same as MR layout RWD LSDs, especially the torque-biasing kind.<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P><P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000009||cadero2dmax||12-10-2001||05:30 PM||||The Python tranny was one they installed a Thorsen from the Rocketparts tranny into a 3.65:1 ratio tranny (htey didn't want the 3.94 ratio, the 4.9 had to much torque for that tranny, in their estimate. <P>It wasn't the diffy that blew, it was the tranny - from what I understand third handedly.<P>I have never had a problem with my Rocketparts Thorsen-Gleason in any fashion or form, except the "push". Conversations with Pantera and Porsche drivers have left me with the opinion that the push is neither Fiero specific nor LSD type/brand specific. <P>Instead "push " with an LSD is rear and mid engine specific, as all had the same opinion and results as I have relayed here. That is not to say that the benefits outweigh the liabilities, but the push is very real. I think, in my car, it would perform better (except at the strip and launch in a straight line) without a limited slip.<P>That is why, even though I am not an auto tranny type of guy, the Challenge car will be TH125 driven. The 2.84 final ratio, the slippage of the tranny, and the action of the torque converter all match up to the 4.9's torque better, IMO.<P>G <P>||||reg||1
